# Agent Guidelines for `information-elements`

## Repository overview
Monorepo for the Information Elements application:
- `ife-gui`: React 19 + TypeScript frontend (Vite, Vitest, ESLint)
- `ife-service`: Spring Boot 4.1.x / Java 21 backend (Maven, JPA, Liquibase, OpenAPI generation, PostgreSQL)

Default branch is `develop`. Feature branches are created from `develop` manually.

## Role
Act as a senior full-stack engineer. Prefer the simplest correct solution. Java 21 now, possibly 25 later: avoid deprecated APIs and use modern Java where it fits (records, switch expressions, pattern matching).

## Before changing any file
1. Find the root cause, not just the symptom.
2. Check what else uses this code (callers, APIs, DB, UI).
3. Confirm the change won't break existing functionality.
4. Make the smallest safe change. No unrelated refactors or formatting churn.
5. Keep frontend and backend separate unless the task crosses both.
6. Preserve existing style, naming, and public APIs.
7. If unsure, inspect the files or ask before editing.

## Code style
- Clean, readable, human-style code. No over-engineering.
- Short comments only where the "why" isn't obvious.
- Each REST endpoint gets a brief Javadoc or OpenAPI description (purpose, request, response, errors).

## Commands
Frontend (`ife-gui`): `npm run lint`, `npm run test`, `npm run build`, `npm run i18n:check`
Backend (`ife-service`): `./mvnw test`, `./mvnw clean package`

## After changing code
- Run the most targeted lint/test/build for the area changed. Run the full set for shared or risky changes.
- Add or update tests for each change (happy path and edge cases).
- Frontend: if UI text changes, keep i18n keys in parity (`npm run i18n:check`).
- Backend: if API contracts change, check the OpenAPI-generated code. If entities change, add a new Liquibase changeset (PostgreSQL). Never edit an already applied changeset.
- Fix all failures. Never skip or delete tests to make them pass.

## Security (deploys to SDG)
- Check for OWASP Top 10 issues: injection, broken auth, XSS, sensitive data exposure.
- Validate input. Use parameterized queries. Never log secrets or personal data.
- On dependency changes, run `npm audit` and report high/critical findings with the fix.
- Prefer patched versions. Flag major upgrades instead of forcing them.

## Documentation
- Small changes: no doc files, just a short summary in chat.
- Major changes (new feature/API, schema or architecture change): one concise doc, max one page.
- Never bulk-generate docs.

## Never touch
- `.env`, `.env.*`, `application*.yml`, `application*.properties`, `bootstrap.*`
- Any file with passwords, keys, tokens, or connection strings
- CI/CD config, Dockerfiles, Kubernetes manifests, unless explicitly asked

Never print, copy, or hardcode secrets. If a config change seems needed, tell me.

## Multi-task requests
- Make a numbered checklist first and finish every task without stopping to ask between them.
- At the end, list each task as done, partial, or not done. Never skip silently.

## Safety
- Don't commit, push, or create branches. I handle all git operations myself.
- No destructive commands (delete, force push, DB drops) without approval.
- No new dependencies without explaining why.

## Notes
- The README references Playwright as the primary end-to-end test framework.
- Keep any new guidance in this file short and actionable.
